infographic list-column-simple-vertical-arrow
data
  title Three flags and one audit: the release-build discipline
  desc Every shipped binary makes three implicit claims — same commit, readable version, identical to what CI verified. None are true by default.
  lists
    - label -trimpath — reproducible, provenance-grade builds
      desc Strips the builder's filesystem paths so identical source and toolchain yield a bit-identical binary on any machine. Costs nothing; the precondition for trusting that a binary maps to a commit. Always on.
    - label -ldflags -s -w — 25-30% smaller binary
      desc Drops the ELF symbol table and DWARF; a 9 MB service lands near 6.5 MB. Panics still symbolize via the runtime pclntab — delve and gdb go blind. Strip only if you archive the unstripped twin.
    - label -X main.version=v1.42.0 — the human version stamp
      desc Injects the version a string variable reads at link time. Complements vcs.revision, vcs.time, and vcs.modified, which Go embeds automatically since 1.18 even with no -X.
    - label go version -m — provenance audit of any binary
      desc Reads module path, full dependency list with hashes, CGO_ENABLED, and the VCS revision off any binary — even one copied out of a running container. The 3 a.m. what-is-running question becomes one command.
theme
  palette #6366f1 #14b8a6 #f59e0b #f43f5e
